WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.459 --> 00:00:04.580
Acting is a job, and like any job, there are
days where you're just going through the motions

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:06.710
to get that sweet, sweet paycheck.

00:00:06.710 --> 00:00:10.120
So whether it's actors who were slumming it
for the money or just doing some work until

00:00:10.120 --> 00:00:13.809
the next prestige project came along, we've
got a look at some stars who were just too

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:15.440
good for their movies.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:17.630
Michael Caine in Jaws: The Revenge

00:00:17.630 --> 00:00:21.749
When he was asked to appear in the fourth
installment of the once-proud Jaws franchise,

00:00:21.749 --> 00:00:25.919
Roy Scheider is rumored to have said, quote,
"Satan himself could not get me to do Jaws

00:00:25.919 --> 00:00:26.999
Part 4."

00:00:26.999 --> 00:00:31.179
Believe it or not, the actual premise of this
movie is that a great white shark is exploiting

00:00:31.179 --> 00:00:35.789
a completely unexplained psychic connection
to Ellen Brody in order to get revenge for

00:00:35.789 --> 00:00:41.980
the death of the shark in the original Jaws,
hence the tagline: "this time, it’s personal."

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:46.550
Michael Caine, who plays a character named
Hoagie, missed out on receiving his Academy

00:00:46.550 --> 00:00:50.590
Award for Best Supporting Actor in Hannah
and Her Sisters — the first Oscar he actually

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.769
won after three nominations — because he
was on location for reshoots to Jaws 4.

00:00:55.769 --> 00:00:59.300
When asked about the film later, he had the
right perspective on the whole thing: "I have

00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.949
never seen it, but by all accounts it is terrible.

00:01:01.949 --> 00:01:05.740
However, I have seen the house that it built,
and it is terrific."

00:01:05.740 --> 00:01:08.299
Jeremy Irons in Dungeons &amp; Dragons

00:01:08.299 --> 00:01:12.860
Academy Award-winner Jeremy Irons is a natural
villain, even when playing an animated lion

00:01:12.860 --> 00:01:14.820
in a Disney movie for babies.

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:18.460
"Long live the king."

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.479
When he played Profion, the villainous wizard
of the 2000 Dungeons &amp; Dragons movie...well,

00:01:23.479 --> 00:01:26.950
let's just say he rolled a Natural 1 on his
Subtlety check.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:30.470
Rather than attempting to bring all the fun
stuff about the game to life, the people behind

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:35.130
the film ended up with a monumentally generic
fantasy setting where the most likable character

00:01:35.130 --> 00:01:38.479
was Marlon Wayans as a cowardly thief named
"Snails."

00:01:38.479 --> 00:01:40.840
But Irons doesn't let the material drag him
down.

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:43.380
His performance is a master class in scenery-chewing.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:49.580
"Let their blood rain from the sky!"

00:01:50.310 --> 00:01:52.200
He's clearly having fun.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.100
Even if the audience isn't.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:56.509
Sir Ben Kingsley in Bloodrayne

00:01:56.509 --> 00:02:02.400
In 1982, Sir Ben Kingsley starred in Gandhi,
a film that was nominated for 11 Academy Awards

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:05.780
and won eight, including Best Actor for Kingsley.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:10.230
Twenty-three years later, Kingsley joined
a cast that included Billy Zane and Meat Loaf

00:02:10.230 --> 00:02:14.640
for BloodRayne, the film adaptation of a video
game that isn't even the fifth best movie

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:15.690
about a half-vampire.

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:20.280
It’s another fine entry in the canon of
Uwe Boll, the director responsible for a string

00:02:20.280 --> 00:02:24.890
of notoriously terrible video game flicks
that were essentially made to exploit a loophole

00:02:24.890 --> 00:02:26.670
in German tax law.

00:02:26.670 --> 00:02:29.300
That said, Kingsley doesn't exactly help matters.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:33.260
While he's clearly capable of much more, he
plays the villain of the film as cold and

00:02:33.260 --> 00:02:34.260
emotionless —

00:02:34.260 --> 00:02:37.870
"With the talisman joined, vampires shall
rule the Earth."

00:02:37.870 --> 00:02:41.090
— which is a nice way of saying that it
seems like everything he says is read from

00:02:41.090 --> 00:02:43.250
a script he's seeing for the first time.

00:02:43.250 --> 00:02:45.320
William Marshall in Blacula

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.860
William Marshall was a classically trained
Shakespearean actor who was once hailed by

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:52.220
the London Sunday Times as "the best Othello
of our time."

00:02:52.220 --> 00:02:57.070
Unfortunately, he’s far more famous for
playing the title role in 1972's Blacula and

00:02:57.070 --> 00:03:01.020
its amazingly titled sequel, Scream Blacula
Scream.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.560
"You shall be…

00:03:04.600 --> 00:03:05.760
Blacula!"

00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:06.940
"Blacula!"

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:12.480
Now, make no mistake: Blacula is actually
a pretty awesome movie, and even stands as

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:16.120
one of the first horror movies to star a black
actor in the lead role.

00:03:16.120 --> 00:03:19.880
Marshall's performance as Mamuwalde — an
African prince who strikes a deal with Dracula

00:03:19.880 --> 00:03:23.790
and ends up as a vampire himself — is downright
commanding.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:27.900
Marshall reportedly insisted that the character
be written with dignity rather than as a bumbling

00:03:27.900 --> 00:03:28.900
punchline.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:33.040
It was even commercially successful, riding
the early '70s Blaxploitation movement to

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.370
become one of 1972's highest-grossing films.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:40.151
That said, it's still a movie in which his
performance is elevating the material, rather

00:03:40.151 --> 00:03:41.970
than the other way around.

00:03:41.970 --> 00:03:44.860
Cate Blanchett in Indiana Jones and the Kingdom
of the Crystal Skull

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:46.950
Well, this is embarrassing.

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.250
It looks like someone made a mistake in the
notes for this video and accidentally wrote

00:03:50.250 --> 00:03:54.360
that there was a fourth Indiana Jones movie
where Indy fights aliens and has a son played

00:03:54.360 --> 00:03:58.690
by Shia LaBeouf, even though we’re pretty
sure there were only three movies in that

00:03:58.690 --> 00:03:59.690
series.

00:03:59.690 --> 00:04:04.220
If, however, there was a fourth Indiana Jones
movie, then Cate Blanchett would've definitely

00:04:04.220 --> 00:04:09.930
been too good for it, especially if was released
in 2008, well after Blanchett had won an Academy

00:04:09.930 --> 00:04:10.930
Award for Best Actress.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:14.820
Then again, everyone in such a movie would've
been slumming it, which is probably why it

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:16.480
definitely never happened.

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:17.480
Ever.

00:04:17.480 --> 00:04:19.769
Bob Hoskins in Super Mario Bros.

00:04:19.769 --> 00:04:23.400
Super Mario Bros. should've worked, and Bob
Hoskins is almost perfect for the part of

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:24.400
Mario.

00:04:24.400 --> 00:04:28.070
Admittedly, he's not Italian, and like any
actor, he was probably very nervous about

00:04:28.070 --> 00:04:31.900
stepping into the role so brilliantly defined
by "Captain" Lou Albano.

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:36.080
"Time for a new day."

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:40.320
Hoskins’ performance in Who Framed Roger
Rabbit is brilliant, and If he can pull that

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.669
off, then pretending to stomp on Koopa Troopas
should be a piece of cake, right?

00:04:44.669 --> 00:04:46.020
Unfortunately, that's not what happened.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:50.180
Mario Bros. is a pretty difficult story to
screw up, but the filmmakers somehow managed

00:04:50.180 --> 00:04:54.050
to drop the ball so hard that it registered
on the Richter scale.

00:04:54.050 --> 00:04:58.620
Instead of a fun romp, it’s an incomprehensible
modern dystopia, full of terrifying nightmare

00:04:58.620 --> 00:04:59.620
monsters.

00:04:59.620 --> 00:05:02.090
And also Dennis Hopper in a mud bath for some
reason.

00:05:02.090 --> 00:05:05.719
"Do you know what I love about mud?

00:05:05.719 --> 00:05:09.349
It's clean, it's dirty at the same time."

00:05:09.349 --> 00:05:11.259
Raul Julia in Street Fighter

00:05:11.259 --> 00:05:16.090
Street Fighter might not be a "good" movie
in the traditional sense, but it's also ridiculously

00:05:16.090 --> 00:05:17.090
fun.

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:21.870
And Raul Julia's genuinely amazing portrayal
of M. Bison is responsible for about 99 percent

00:05:21.870 --> 00:05:22.870
of that.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:29.039
"For I beheld Satan as he fell from heaven!!"

00:05:29.039 --> 00:05:32.849
Saying he's going over the top with his performance
doesn't quite cover it, because that implies

00:05:32.849 --> 00:05:35.939
that he's somehow going beyond what the material
demands.

00:05:35.939 --> 00:05:37.550
That's not what's happening here at all.

00:05:37.550 --> 00:05:42.060
The movie's version of Bison is the kind of
megalomaniac who has an entire financial plan

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.270
based on printing up his own money and then
kidnapping the Queen of England in order to

00:05:46.270 --> 00:05:47.939
set up the exchange rate.

00:05:47.939 --> 00:05:52.430
Sadly, Street Fighter was also Julia's last
movie, a part he took despite suffering from

00:05:52.430 --> 00:05:55.690
stomach cancer because his children were fans
of the game.

00:05:55.690 --> 00:05:59.719
But while his death at the height of his career
is truly tragic, Julia's performance is beautifully,

00:05:59.719 --> 00:06:00.719
unironically fantastic.

00:06:00.719 --> 00:06:06.639
For fans of schlock, it's one of the most
fun, self-aware, and endlessly quotable performances

00:06:06.639 --> 00:06:08.939
ever to grace a goofy video game adaptation.

00:06:08.939 --> 00:06:11.160
But for Raul Julia…

00:06:11.160 --> 00:06:13.169
"It was Tuesday."

00:06:13.169 --> 00:06:15.090
Sir Patrick Stewart in The Emoji Movie

00:06:15.090 --> 00:06:17.289
Finally, there's this.

00:06:17.289 --> 00:06:18.689
You had to know this was coming.

00:06:18.689 --> 00:06:23.409
Sir Patrick Stewart, legendary Shakespearean
actor, famous for bringing Captain Jean-Luc

00:06:23.409 --> 00:06:28.900
Picard and Professor X, to life, ...star of
stage and screen, patron saint of pop culture,

00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:31.439
and all around super-talented guy…

00:06:31.439 --> 00:06:34.750
literally plays a talking pile of poop in
a movie about emojis.

00:06:34.750 --> 00:06:36.900
"This is such a load of…"

00:06:38.180 --> 00:06:39.180
"No, go ahead.

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:41.460
Finish that sentence."

00:06:41.460 --> 00:06:43.510
There's literally nothing to add to that.

00:06:43.510 --> 00:06:44.879
Thanks for watching!

00:06:44.879 --> 00:06:47.289
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:47.289 --> 00:06:49.490
Plus check out all this other cool stuff we
know you'll love too.

